    figure: { src: '/exam-figures/geography/geography-2023-hl-rock-cycle-diagram.png', alt: 'Cross-section diagram of the rock cycle. On the left a volcano erupts lava, fed by a column of red Magma rising from \'Magma from molten crust and mantle\' deep below. A numbered box 3 sits on the rising-magma path beside the green \'Igneous rock\' label. At the top centre, a numbered box 1 sits near the volcano summit and \'Slow uplift to surface\', with an arrow labelled \'Transport and deposition\' leading right toward \'Sedimentation\' at the sea. On the right, a numbered box 2 sits beside the green \'Sedimentary rock\' label. Lower down, a \'Burial, high temperatures and pressures\' caption sits on the path leading to the green \'Metamorphic rock\' label, with \'Melting\' returning material toward the magma. Source note: Amended from www.mylearning.org.', source: 'SEC Leaving Certificate Geography 2023 Higher Level, Paper 1, Q4 — © State Examinations Commission' },
