    gist: 'A budget is a plan for how a household spends its income so that spending never exceeds money coming in. The advantages: it develops good money-management skills, identifies areas of overspending, reduces reliance on credit, provides financial security, reduces stress, covers unplanned events/emergencies and encourages people to wait until they have the money before buying (avoiding impulse buying). A weekly budget plan splits NET income (take-home pay) across categories — housing/rent/mortgage 25%, food 25%, household bills (lighting, heating, bin collection, phone, internet) 15%, education 10%, clothes 5%, leisure/entertainment 5%, savings 5%, health 5%, discretionary 5% — and is evaluated/reviewed on a regular basis. If a household runs into debt, MABS (the Money Advice and Budgeting Service) gives a FREE, independent advice service — it advises on coping with debt, helps plan budgets and repayment schedules, advises on consumer finance products and interest rates, and negotiates with lenders. MABS does NOT lend money.',
