    gist: 'The Consumer Protection Act 2007 protects shoppers by prohibiting a trader from making false or misleading claims. It bans false or misleading claims about GOODS (e.g. their nature, quality, quantity or whether they are new), about SERVICES (e.g. how, when or by whom they\'re provided) and about PRICE — including misleading \'previous price\' (was/now) claims and false \'recommended retail price\' (RRP) claims. Enforcement is carried out by a statutory body, the CCPC (Competition and Consumer Protection Commission), which can investigate complaints and take action against traders. Sitting alongside it, VOLUNTARY consumer agencies help too: the Consumers\' Association of Ireland (CAI) carries out research and surveys on goods and services published in Consumer Choice, gives objective information and lobbies government; the Advertising Standards Authority of Ireland (ASAI) promotes high standards in advertising, ensures ads are legal, truthful, honest and decent, and can instruct a company to modify or remove an advertisement.',
