    id: 'he-ol-consumer-complaint-redress',
    subjectId: 'home-economics', topicId: 'home-economics-1-2',
    subjectLabel: 'Home Economics', topicLabel: 'Consumer Studies',
    focus: 'Complaint procedure & Small Claims Court', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'When you buy goods, you have consumer RIGHTS: the right to value for money, to honest and truthful information, to a choice of goods and services, to quality and safety, and the right to redress if something goes wrong. Redress means putting it right — a repair, a refund or a replacement. If a product is faulty, follow a clear COMPLAINT PROCEDURE. (1) Return to the retailer (the shop you bought it from), bringing the receipt as proof of purchase. (2) Complain promptly — speak to the manager, outline the complaint and state what you want done (repair, refund or replacement). (3) If the response is unsatisfactory, put the complaint IN WRITING — the letter should describe the goods/service, include a copy of the receipt, say where and when it was bought, and state the redress you are seeking. (4) If it is still not resolved, you can take the case to the SMALL CLAIMS COURT. The Small Claims procedure is part of the District Court office: you fill in an application form and pay a small fee (€25) to have it processed; the claim is registered and a notice is sent to the trader (the respondent), who has 15 days to reply (admit, dispute, ignore or counter-claim); the small claims registrar tries to resolve it, and if it cannot be resolved it may be referred to a court hearing. It handles claims up to €2,000.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Give the complaint as an ordered PROCEDURE — retailer + receipt first, Small Claims last',
      text: 'The 2022 OL scheme marks the complaint procedure as 5 points @ 3 marks (graded 3:2:0), and the 2024 OL Small Claims question as 4 points @ 5 marks (graded 5:3:0). Students lose marks by jumping straight to \'go to court\' or \'ring a solicitor\'. The scheme rewards the correct ORDER: return to the retailer with the receipt, complain in person to the manager, then in writing, and only register with the Small Claims Court if it is still unresolved. Always name \'receipt / proof of purchase\' and the redress sought (repair, refund or replacement) — these are scoring points, not filler.',
    },
