    subjectLabel: 'Home Economics', topicLabel: 'Household Technology & Energy',
    focus: 'Choosing appliances & the EU energy label', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Household technology (washing machines, fridges, kettles, hobs) makes managing the home easier — it reduces the workload, saves time and lets tasks be done more efficiently. But appliances cost money to buy and to run, so you must CHOOSE wisely. Factors to consider when buying a kitchen appliance: cost (price to buy), energy efficiency (how much electricity it uses), safety, the size of the family and the size/shape of the appliance, ease of use and cleaning/maintenance, the guarantee and after-sales service, the brand, and any special features. The EU ENERGY LABEL is the key tool here: it rates appliances on an A-G scale (A and B rated are the most energy efficient), so it lets the consumer compare appliances on their energy use, choose a more energy-efficient model that will REDUCE energy bills, pick a more sustainable product that has less impact on the environment, and (on newer labels) scan a QR code to a database for more details. To run a home cheaply and safely: use energy-efficient (A-rated) appliances, don\'t leave appliances on standby, use timers and thermostats, fix dripping taps, and use LED bulbs — and use appliances safely by following the manufacturer\'s instructions, not overloading sockets and not touching them with wet hands.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'When you \'choose\' an appliance, name DISTINCT factors — and tie the EU label to A-G + lower bills',
      text: 'The 2024 OL scheme awards \'choose a kitchen appliance\' as 3 factors @ 5 marks (graded 5:3:0) and the 2022 OL EU energy label question as 2 benefits @ 5 marks (graded 5:3:0). Students lose marks by repeating the same idea twice (\'it\'s cheap\' and \'good value\') or by describing what an appliance does instead of why you\'d choose it. For the energy label, the scheme rewards saying it rates appliances on an A-G scale (A/B = most efficient) AND that this lets the consumer reduce energy bills / choose a more sustainable, environmentally friendly product — naming the benefit, not just \'it shows energy\'. Develop each point into a sentence.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'A family is buying a new washing machine. (i) Discuss three factors that should be considered when choosing the appliance. (ii) Explain two benefits to the consumer of the EU energy label on electrical appliances.',
      modelAnswer: '(i) Three factors: COST — the family should consider both the purchase price and how much it costs to run, choosing one within their budget; ENERGY EFFICIENCY — choose a machine that uses less electricity so it is cheaper to run; and SIZE/SUITABILITY for the family — a larger family needs a bigger drum capacity, and the machine must fit the available space, with a guarantee and good after-sales service. (ii) Two benefits of the EU energy label: it rates appliances on an A-G scale (A and B are the most energy efficient), so the consumer can compare models and choose the most efficient one, which helps reduce their energy bills; and choosing a more energy-efficient, sustainable product reduces the appliance\'s impact on the environment.',
      needed: ['Three distinct factors for choosing the appliance (e.g. cost, energy efficiency, size/suitability for the family, guarantee/after-sales)', 'Each factor developed into a sentence, not a one-word list (3 @ 5 marks, 5:3:0)', 'EU label benefit 1: rates appliances A-G (A/B most efficient) so the consumer can compare and reduce energy bills', 'EU label benefit 2: helps choose a more sustainable product, reducing impact on the environment', 'Do not repeat the same point twice'],
    },
    source: 'LC Home Economics — Resource Management & Consumer Studies (Household Technology & Energy). Marking schemes: 2024 Ordinary Level, Section C Core (Q4) Q4(b)(i) — three factors when choosing a kitchen appliance (3 @ 5 marks, 5:3:0) and Q4(b)(ii) — safe use of electrical kitchen appliances (3 @ 5 marks); 2022 Ordinary Level, Section B Q4(c) — two benefits of the EU energy label (2 @ 5 marks, 5:3:0; A-G scale, reduces energy bills, sustainability/QR database), Section C Elective 1 Q1(b)(ii) — three ways to reduce home energy bills (A-rated appliances, no standby, timers/thermostats, LED bulbs; 3 @ 5 marks), and Section C Core Q4(c)(ii) — three ways technology contributes to efficient management of the home (reduces workload, saves time, increased efficiency; 3 @ 5 marks)',
    marksWeight: 25,
